        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "caef395f-e88e-5802-b35b-3135eebe3677",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Neil B. Shulman, Michael Caton-Jones, Jeffrey Price, Peter S. Seaman, Dallas Hall, Elijah Saunders\n- Works: Doc Hollywood, What? Dead...Again?\n- Organizations: International Society on Hypertension in Blacks\n- Topics: Hypertension among African Americans, minority health\n\nFacts:\n- In 1986, Dallas Hall, Neil B. Shulman, and Elijah Saunders founded the International Society on Hypertension in Blacks in response to concern about high rates of hypertension among African Americans.\n- In 1991, the American romantic comedy film Doc Hollywood was directed by Michael Caton-Jones and written by Jeffrey Price and Peter S. Seaman.\n- In 1991, Doc Hollywood was based on Neil B. Shulman’s book What? Dead...Again?.\n- Neil B. Shulman was an associate producer of the 1991 film Doc Hollywood, which was based on one of his books.\n- By 2006, the International Society on Hypertension in Blacks had broadened its scope to focus on improving the health of all minority populations around the world, not only on reducing hypertension among African Americans.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
